module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.EtaleCohomology.ConstantSheafVanishingTransport
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris

/-!
# Mayer--Vietoris in the absolute étale cohomology of the open subschemes (Stacks 0A50)

`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence` proves the long exact
Mayer--Vietoris sequence of two open subschemes `U`, `V` of a scheme `X` with terms the
cohomology `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.H'` of an abelian sheaf `F` over the four *objects* `U ⊓ V`,
`U`, `V`, `U ⊔ V` of the small étale site of `X` — the `Ext`-groups out of the free abelian
sheaf generated by those objects. [Stacks, Tag 0A50] names instead the absolute étale cohomology
groups `Hⁿ(U ∩ V, F)`, `Hⁿ(U, F)`, `Hⁿ(V, F)`, `Hⁿ(X, F)` of the open subschemes themselves.
This file identifies the two and restates the tag with the groups the source names.

The identification holds for every open subscheme and uses nothing of the Mayer--Vietoris square:
for `W` an open subscheme of `X`, the cohomology of `F` over `W` read as an object of the small
étale site of `X` is the global cohomology of the restriction of `F` to the small étale site of
the scheme `W`.

Previously only the ambient term of the sequence was identified, and only under the source's
hypothesis `U ∪ V = X`: `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySupIsoH` identifies
`Hⁿ(U ⊔ V, F)` with the global cohomology of `F` on the small étale site of `X` itself when
`U ⊔ V = ⊤`, that object being then terminal. That is a different identification of the same
group from the one proved here, which goes through the small étale site of the open subscheme
`U ⊔ V` and needs no hypothesis; neither is deduced from the other. Their composite
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.supIsoGlobalH` is what carries the sequence from the
étale cohomology of the open subscheme `U ∪ V` to the global étale cohomology of `X`, and so
from the general statement to the source's own.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.opensRestriction`: the restriction `F ↦ F|_W` of an
  abelian sheaf on the small étale site of `X` to the small étale site of an open subscheme `W`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.absoluteSequence` and `.absoluteInitialSequence`: the
  Mayer--Vietoris sequence and its initial segment, with the absolute étale cohomology groups of
  `U ⊔ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ⊓ V` as their terms.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.absoluteDelta`: the connecting morphism
  `Hⁿ⁰(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ∪ V, F)` of that sequence.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.coverSequence` and `.coverInitialSequence`: the same
  sequence and initial segment under the source's hypothesis `U ⊔ V = ⊤`, with the ambient terms
  the global étale cohomology groups `Hⁿ(X, F)` of `X` itself, together with their maps
  `.coverToBiprod` and `.coverDelta`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.h'OpensIsoH`, `h'OpensAddEquivH` and the functor form
  `cohomologyOpensFunctorIsoH`: **the comparison.** For an open subscheme `W` of `X`, an abelian
  sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X` and a degree `n`,

  `Hⁿ(W, F) ≅ Hⁿ(W_étale, F|_W)`,

  naturally in `F`: the cohomology of `F` over `W` as an object of the small étale site of `X` is
  the absolute étale cohomology of the scheme `W` with coefficients in the restriction of `F`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.absoluteSequence_exact` and
  `.absoluteInitialSequence_exact`: the sequence of absolute étale cohomology groups is exact, in
  every six-term window and at its initial segment.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.absoluteDelta_naturality`,
  `.absoluteToBiprod_naturality` and `.absoluteFromBiprod_naturality`: every map of the sequence
  — the connecting morphisms, the sum of the two restriction maps and their difference — is
  natural in the abelian sheaf, so a morphism of abelian sheaves induces a morphism of long exact
  sequences.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`:
  **the whole of [Stacks, Tag 0A50]**, the conjunction of the results above, stated with
  the étale cohomology groups of the open subschemes. This carries the registration of the tag.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.supIsoGlobalH` and `.supIsoGlobalH_naturality`: for
  `U ⊔ V = ⊤` the ambient term `Hⁿ(U ∪ V, F)` is the global étale cohomology `Hⁿ(X, F)`,
  naturally in `F`.
* `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_cover`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0A50] as the source states it**, under the hypothesis `X = U ∪ V` and with the
  ambient terms the global étale cohomology groups of `X`. This too carries the registration of
  the tag: the tag's content is the union of what the declarations carrying it say, and the
  statement above is strictly more general than the source's.

## Implementation notes

Both halves of the comparison were already in the library and are used here, not reproved.

The change of site is [Stacks, Tag 03YU] statement 2,
`Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSite.cohomologyPresheafFunctorIso`: for a continuous and cocontinuous
functor of essentially small sites preserving fibre products and equalizers, restriction of
abelian sheaves does not change the cohomology over an object. The functor is
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.ConstantTransport.opensInclusion`, composition with the open immersion
read on the one-universe models of the two small étale sites, whose continuity, cocontinuity and
preservation of fibre products and equalizers are registered there.

The passage from cohomology over an object to global cohomology is
`Stacks.Cohomology.TerminalCohomology.functorHIso` at the object `W / W` of the small étale site
of `W`, terminal by `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.selfIsTerminal`; that object is carried
to `W` read in the small étale site of `X` by
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.ConstantTransport.opensInclusionSelfIso`. The comparison is assembled at
the level of functors of the abelian sheaf, in `cohomologyOpensFunctorIsoH`, so that its
naturality in `F` — which is what the functoriality clause of the source needs — is
`CategoryTheory.NatTrans.naturality` and costs no separate argument.

The four comparisons used in the sequence are stated at the objects of the Mayer--Vietoris square
rather than at `(opensToSite X).obj W`. The two spellings are definitionally equal, but `simp`
matches objects only up to reducible unfolding, so a composite mixing them is not recognised as a
composite of an isomorphism with its inverse and the transport of the sequence does not go
through.

**What is identified and what is not.** The identification is of the terms. The arrows of
`absoluteSequence` are *defined* to be the restriction maps and the connecting maps of the small
étale site of `X` read through the comparison isomorphisms, which is what makes the exactness of
the sequence of absolute groups follow from the exactness already proved. Identifying those
arrows with the restriction maps of the absolute cohomology of the open subschemes — the maps the
source writes down — is a further statement, requiring the comparison to be natural in the open
subscheme `W`; that is a compatibility of
`Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSite.freeYonedaSheafPullbackIso` with the factorisation
`opensInclusion X W' = opensInclusion W W' ⋙ opensInclusion X W` of the inclusion functors, and
it is **not** proved here.

`mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology` makes no hypothesis `U ⊔ V = ⊤`, so it is more
general than the source in the same way `mayerVietoris_longExactSequence` is: its ambient term is
the absolute étale cohomology of the open subscheme `U ⊔ V`, which for `U ⊔ V = ⊤` is `X` itself.
`mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_cover` is the source's own statement, obtained from it by
transporting the terms along `supIsoGlobalH`; the exactness clauses go across by
`CategoryTheory.ComposableArrows.exact_of_iso` and the three functoriality clauses by naturality
of that comparison in the sheaf. The clause the source's shape needs that the general statement
does not is naturality of `supIsoGlobalH` in `F`, which is naturality of
`cohomologySupIsoH` — cohomology over a terminal object is global cohomology as functors of the
sheaf — composed with `cohomologyX₄IsoH_naturality`. No naturality of the comparison in the open
subscheme is needed, and none is available; see above.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0A50](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0A50)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03YU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03YU)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Abelian AlgebraicGeometry Opposite

universe u

namespace Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris

/-! ### Cohomology over an open subscheme is the étale cohomology of that subscheme -/

section Restriction

variable (X : Scheme.{u}) (W : X.Opens)

/-- **The restriction `F ↦ F|_W` of an abelian sheaf on the small étale site of `X` to the small
étale site of an open subscheme `W`**: the direct image along composition with the open immersion
`W ⟶ X`, read on the one-universe models of the two sites. -/
abbrev opensRestriction :
    Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} ⥤
      Sheaf (topology W.toScheme) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} :=
  (ConstantTransport.opensInclusion X W).sheafPushforwardContinuous AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}
    (topology W.toScheme) (topology X)

/-- The degree-`n` étale cohomology of the scheme `W`, as a functor of an abelian sheaf on the
small étale site of `W`. -/
abbrev opensCohomologyFunctor (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf (topology W.toScheme) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} ⥤ AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} :=
  Sheaf.functorH.{u + 1, u + 1, u + 1, u + 1} (topology W.toScheme) n

/-- **The map `Hⁿ(W, F|_W) ⟶ Hⁿ(W, G|_W)` on the étale cohomology of `W`** induced by a morphism
`F ⟶ G` of abelian sheaves on the small étale site of `X`.

This is `opensCohomologyFunctor` acting on a morphism; it is given a name so that its source and
target are spelled as the groups `Hⁿ` themselves, which is what lets the composites below be
recognised by `simp`. -/
def opensCohomologyMap (n : ℕ) {F G : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}} (f : F ⟶ G) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X W).obj F) n) ⟶
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X W).obj G) n) :=
  (opensCohomologyFunctor X W n).map ((opensRestriction X W).map f)

/-- **Cohomology over an open subscheme does not change under restriction to it.** The cohomology
of `F|_W` over the object `W / W` of the small étale site of `W` is the cohomology of `F` over the
image of that object in the small étale site of `X`, naturally in `F`.

This is [Stacks, Tag 03YU] statement 2 at the functor
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.ConstantTransport.opensInclusion`. -/
def cohomologyOpensFunctorIso (n : ℕ) :
    opensRestriction X W ⋙ Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology W.toScheme) n ⋙
        (evaluation (Site W.toScheme)ᵒᵖ AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}).obj
          (op (self W.toScheme)) ≅
      Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n ⋙
        (evaluation (Site X)ᵒᵖ AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}).obj
          (op ((ConstantTransport.opensInclusion X W).obj (self W.toScheme))) :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSite.cohomologyPresheafFunctorIso
    (topology W.toScheme) (topology X) (ConstantTransport.opensInclusion X W)
    (self W.toScheme) n

/-- **The comparison, as functors of the abelian sheaf.** Evaluating the degree-`n` cohomology
presheaf of the small étale site of `X` at the open subscheme `W` is the degree-`n` étale
cohomology of the scheme `W` composed with restriction to `W`.

The two steps are [Stacks, Tag 03YU] statement 2, that restriction along composition with the
open immersion does not change cohomology over an object (`cohomologyOpensFunctorIso`), and the
fact that `W / W` is a terminal object of the small étale site of `W`, so that cohomology over it
is global cohomology (`Stacks.Cohomology.TerminalCohomology.functorHIso`). -/
def cohomologyOpensFunctorIsoH (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n ⋙
        (evaluation (Site X)ᵒᵖ AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}).obj (op ((opensToSite X).obj W)) ≅
      opensRestriction X W ⋙ opensCohomologyFunctor X W n :=
  Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n)
      ((evaluation (Site X)ᵒᵖ AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}).mapIso
        (ConstantTransport.opensInclusionSelfIso X W).op) ≪≫
    (cohomologyOpensFunctorIso X W n).symm ≪≫
    Functor.isoWhiskerLeft (opensRestriction X W)
      (Stacks.Cohomology.TerminalCohomology.functorHIso (topology W.toScheme)
        (self W.toScheme) (selfIsTerminal W.toScheme) n)

/-- **The comparison: cohomology over an open subscheme is the étale cohomology of that open
subscheme.** For an open subscheme `W` of a scheme `X`, an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale
site of `X` and a degree `n`,

`Hⁿ(W, F) ≅ Hⁿ(W_étale, F|_W)`,

the left-hand side being the cohomology of `F` over `W` read as an object of the small étale site
of `X` and the right-hand side the global étale cohomology of the scheme `W` with coefficients in
the restriction of `F`. -/
def h'OpensIsoH (F : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n ((opensToSite X).obj W) ≅
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X W).obj F) n) :=
  (cohomologyOpensFunctorIsoH X W n).app F

/-- The comparison `Hⁿ(W, F) ≅ Hⁿ(W_étale, F|_W)` of
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.h'OpensIsoH`, read as an additive equivalence of the
underlying groups. -/
def h'OpensAddEquivH (F : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n ((opensToSite X).obj W) ≃+
      Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X W).obj F) n :=
  (h'OpensIsoH X W F n).addCommGroupIsoToAddEquiv

/-- **The comparison is natural in the abelian sheaf**: the restriction maps of the cohomology
over `W` correspond to the maps induced on the étale cohomology of the scheme `W`. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem h'OpensIsoH_naturality {F G : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}} (f : F ⟶ G)
    (n : ℕ) :
    ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app
          (op ((opensToSite X).obj W)) ≫ (h'OpensIsoH X W G n).hom =
      (h'OpensIsoH X W F n).hom ≫ opensCohomologyMap X W n f :=
  (cohomologyOpensFunctorIsoH X W n).hom.naturality f

end Restriction


/-! ### The four terms of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence -/

section Square

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} (F G : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (U V : X.Opens)

/-- **The ambient term of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is the étale cohomology of `U ∪ V`.** No
hypothesis `U ⊔ V = ⊤` is made. When it does hold, the same group is identified with the global
étale cohomology of `F` on the small étale site of `X` itself by
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySupIsoH`. -/
def cohomologyX₄IsoH (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n (square X U V).X₄ ≅
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊔ V)).obj F) n) :=
  h'OpensIsoH X (U ⊔ V) F n

/-- **The second term of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is the étale cohomology of `U`.** -/
def cohomologyX₂IsoH (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n (square X U V).X₂ ≅
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X U).obj F) n) :=
  h'OpensIsoH X U F n

/-- **The third term of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is the étale cohomology of `V`.** -/
def cohomologyX₃IsoH (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n (square X U V).X₃ ≅
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X V).obj F) n) :=
  h'OpensIsoH X V F n

/-- **The last term of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is the étale cohomology of `U ∩ V`.** -/
def cohomologyX₁IsoH (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n (square X U V).X₁ ≅
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊓ V)).obj F) n) :=
  h'OpensIsoH X (U ⊓ V) F n

/-- The middle term of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is the direct sum of the étale cohomology
groups of `U` and of `V`. -/
def biprodIsoH (n : ℕ) :
    Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n (square X U V).X₂ ⊞ Sheaf.H'.{u + 1} F n (square X U V).X₃ ≅
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X U).obj F) n) ⊞
        AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X V).obj F) n) :=
  biprod.mapIso (cohomologyX₂IsoH F U V n) (cohomologyX₃IsoH F U V n)

/-- The comparison at `U ∩ V` is natural in the abelian sheaf. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem cohomologyX₁IsoH_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₁) ≫
        (cohomologyX₁IsoH G U V n).hom =
      (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n).hom ≫ opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n f :=
  h'OpensIsoH_naturality X (U ⊓ V) f n

/-- The comparison at `U ∪ V` is natural in the abelian sheaf. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem cohomologyX₄IsoH_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₄) ≫
        (cohomologyX₄IsoH G U V n).hom =
      (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n).hom ≫ opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊔ V) n f :=
  h'OpensIsoH_naturality X (U ⊔ V) f n

/-- The comparison at `U` is natural in the abelian sheaf. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem cohomologyX₂IsoH_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₂) ≫
        (cohomologyX₂IsoH G U V n).hom =
      (cohomologyX₂IsoH F U V n).hom ≫ opensCohomologyMap X U n f :=
  h'OpensIsoH_naturality X U f n

/-- The comparison at `V` is natural in the abelian sheaf. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem cohomologyX₃IsoH_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₃) ≫
        (cohomologyX₃IsoH G U V n).hom =
      (cohomologyX₃IsoH F U V n).hom ≫ opensCohomologyMap X V n f :=
  h'OpensIsoH_naturality X V f n

/-- The comparison of the middle terms is natural in the abelian sheaf, being the biproduct of
the comparisons at `U` and at `V`. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem biprodIsoH_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    biprod.map
          (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₂))
          (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app
            (op (square X U V).X₃)) ≫ (biprodIsoH G U V n).hom =
      (biprodIsoH F U V n).hom ≫
        biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f) := by
  refine biprod.hom_ext' _ _ ?_ ?_ <;>
    simp only [biprodIsoH, biprod.mapIso_hom, biprod.inl_map_assoc,
      biprod.inr_map_assoc, biprod.inl_map, biprod.inr_map,
      cohomologyX₂IsoH_naturality_assoc, cohomologyX₃IsoH_naturality_assoc]

/-- **The sum of the two restriction maps is natural in the abelian sheaf.** It is the
`biprod.lift` of two maps of the degree-`n` cohomology presheaf, so this is naturality of that
presheaf in the sheaf, which is `CategoryTheory.NatTrans.naturality` for
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologyX₂IsoH`'s ambient functor. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem toBiprod_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₄) ≫
        toBiprod G U V n =
      toBiprod F U V n ≫
        biprod.map
          (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₂))
          (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app
            (op (square X U V).X₃)) := by
  refine biprod.hom_ext _ _ ?_ ?_
  · simp only [toBiprod, GrothendieckTopology.MayerVietorisSquare.toBiprod, Category.assoc,
      biprod.lift_fst, biprod.map_fst, biprod.lift_fst_assoc]
    exact (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).naturality
      (square X U V).f₂₄.op).symm
  · simp only [toBiprod, GrothendieckTopology.MayerVietorisSquare.toBiprod, Category.assoc,
      biprod.lift_snd, biprod.map_snd, biprod.lift_snd_assoc]
    exact (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).naturality
      (square X U V).f₃₄.op).symm

/-- **The difference of the two restriction maps is natural in the abelian sheaf.** It is the
`biprod.desc` of two maps of the degree-`n` cohomology presheaf, so this too is naturality of
that presheaf in the sheaf. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem fromBiprod_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    biprod.map
          (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₂))
          (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app
            (op (square X U V).X₃)) ≫ fromBiprod G U V n =
      fromBiprod F U V n ≫
        ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₁) := by
  refine biprod.hom_ext' _ _ ?_ ?_
  · simp only [fromBiprod, GrothendieckTopology.MayerVietorisSquare.fromBiprod,
      biprod.inl_map_assoc, biprod.inl_desc, biprod.inl_desc_assoc]
    exact (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).naturality
      (square X U V).f₁₂.op).symm
  · simp only [fromBiprod, GrothendieckTopology.MayerVietorisSquare.fromBiprod,
      biprod.inr_map_assoc, biprod.inr_desc, biprod.inr_desc_assoc]
    rw [Preadditive.comp_neg, Preadditive.neg_comp, neg_inj]
    exact (((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).naturality
      (square X U V).f₁₃.op).symm

end Square

/-! ### The Mayer--Vietoris sequence of étale cohomology groups -/

section Sequence

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} (F G : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (U V : X.Opens)

/-- The sum of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)` of the étale
cohomology of the open subschemes, read through the comparison. -/
def absoluteToBiprod (n : ℕ) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊔ V)).obj F) n) ⟶
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X U).obj F) n) ⊞
        AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X V).obj F) n) :=
  (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n).inv ≫ toBiprod F U V n ≫ (biprodIsoH F U V n).hom

/-- The difference of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U ∩ V, F)` of the étale
cohomology of the open subschemes, read through the comparison. -/
def absoluteFromBiprod (n : ℕ) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X U).obj F) n) ⊞
        AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X V).obj F) n) ⟶
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊓ V)).obj F) n) :=
  (biprodIsoH F U V n).inv ≫ fromBiprod F U V n ≫ (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n).hom

/-- **The connecting morphism `δ : Hⁿ⁰(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ∪ V, F)`** of the Mayer--Vietoris
sequence of étale cohomology groups: the connecting morphism of
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.delta` read through the comparison. -/
def absoluteDelta (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊓ V)).obj F) n₀) ⟶
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊔ V)).obj F) n₁) :=
  (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n₀).inv ≫ delta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫ (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n₁).hom

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris sequence in the étale cohomology of the open subschemes.** For open
subschemes `U`, `V` of `X`, an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X` and
`n₀ + 1 = n₁`, this is the six-term window

`Hⁿ⁰(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁰(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ⁰(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁰(U ∩ V, F)
  ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ¹(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ∩ V, F)`

of the long exact sequence, with the absolute étale cohomology groups of the four open subschemes
as its terms. -/
abbrev absoluteSequence (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    ComposableArrows AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} 5 :=
  ComposableArrows.mk₅ (absoluteToBiprod F U V n₀) (absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₀)
    (absoluteDelta F U V n₀ n₁ h) (absoluteToBiprod F U V n₁) (absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₁)

/-- The comparison of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence over the objects of the small étale site with
the Mayer--Vietoris sequence of the étale cohomology groups of the open subschemes: the terms are
compared by `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.h'OpensIsoH` and the arrows of the second are
by definition those of the first read through that comparison. -/
def absoluteSequenceIso (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    cohomologySequence F U V n₀ n₁ h ≅ absoluteSequence F U V n₀ n₁ h :=
  ComposableArrows.isoMk₅ (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n₀) (biprodIsoH F U V n₀)
    (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n₀) (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n₁) (biprodIsoH F U V n₁)
    (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n₁)
    (by
      change toBiprod F U V n₀ ≫ (biprodIsoH F U V n₀).hom =
        (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n₀).hom ≫ absoluteToBiprod F U V n₀
      simp [absoluteToBiprod])
    (by
      change fromBiprod F U V n₀ ≫ (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n₀).hom =
        (biprodIsoH F U V n₀).hom ≫ absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₀
      simp [absoluteFromBiprod])
    (by
      change delta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫ (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n₁).hom =
        (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n₀).hom ≫ absoluteDelta F U V n₀ n₁ h
      simp [absoluteDelta])
    (by
      change toBiprod F U V n₁ ≫ (biprodIsoH F U V n₁).hom =
        (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n₁).hom ≫ absoluteToBiprod F U V n₁
      simp [absoluteToBiprod])
    (by
      change fromBiprod F U V n₁ ≫ (cohomologyX₁IsoH F U V n₁).hom =
        (biprodIsoH F U V n₁).hom ≫ absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₁
      simp [absoluteFromBiprod])

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris sequence of étale cohomology groups is exact**: for open subschemes
`U`, `V` of a scheme `X` and an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X`, every six-term
window of

`⋯ ⟶ Hⁿ(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁺¹(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ ⋯`

is exact. This is one of the three clauses of [Stacks, Tag 0A50], which is stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`. -/
theorem absoluteSequence_exact (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    (absoluteSequence F U V n₀ n₁ h).Exact :=
  ComposableArrows.exact_of_iso (absoluteSequenceIso F U V n₀ n₁ h)
    (cohomologySequence_exact F U V n₀ n₁ h)

/-- The initial segment

`0 ⟶ H⁰(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U ∩ V, F)
  ⟶ H¹(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ H¹(U, F) ⊞ H¹(V, F) ⟶ H¹(U ∩ V, F)`

of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence of étale cohomology groups, with its leading zero. -/
abbrev absoluteInitialSequence : ComposableArrows AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} 6 :=
  (absoluteSequence F U V 0 1 rfl).precomp
    (0 : AddCommGrpCat.of PUnit.{u + 2} ⟶
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊔ V)).obj F) 0))

/-- The comparison of the two initial segments, extending `absoluteSequenceIso` by the identity
of the leading zero. -/
def absoluteInitialSequenceIso :
    initialSequence F U V ≅ absoluteInitialSequence F U V :=
  ComposableArrows.isoMkSucc (Iso.refl _) (absoluteSequenceIso F U V 0 1 rfl) (by simp)

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris sequence of étale cohomology groups is exact at its initial segment,
leading zero included**: the sum of the two restriction maps
`H⁰(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F)` is injective. This is one of the three clauses of
[Stacks, Tag 0A50], which is stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`. -/
theorem absoluteInitialSequence_exact : (absoluteInitialSequence F U V).Exact :=
  ComposableArrows.exact_of_iso (absoluteInitialSequenceIso F U V)
    (initialSequence_exact F U V)

/-- A square commuting with the forward maps of two isomorphisms commutes with their inverses. -/
private theorem comp_inv_eq_inv_comp_of_comp_hom_eq {C : Type*} [Category C] {A B A' B' : C}
    (eA : A ≅ B) (eB : A' ≅ B') (a : A ⟶ A') (b : B ⟶ B')
    (h : a ≫ eB.hom = eA.hom ≫ b) : b ≫ eB.inv = eA.inv ≫ a := by
  rw [Iso.comp_inv_eq, Category.assoc, Iso.eq_inv_comp]
  exact h.symm

/-- **The sum of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)` of the sequence
of étale cohomology groups is natural in the abelian sheaf.** It is the corresponding map over
the objects of the small étale site, natural by `toBiprod_naturality`, read through the
comparison, natural by `h'OpensIsoH_naturality`. This is part of the functoriality clause of
[Stacks, Tag 0A50], which is stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem absoluteToBiprod_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊔ V) n f ≫ absoluteToBiprod G U V n =
      absoluteToBiprod F U V n ≫
        biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f) := by
  have key := comp_inv_eq_inv_comp_of_comp_hom_eq _ _ _ _
    (cohomologyX₄IsoH_naturality F G U V f n)
  dsimp only [absoluteToBiprod]
  simp only [Category.assoc]
  rw [reassoc_of% key, toBiprod_naturality_assoc, biprodIsoH_naturality]

/-- **The difference of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U ∩ V, F)` of the
sequence of étale cohomology groups is natural in the abelian sheaf.** It is the corresponding
map over the objects of the small étale site, natural by `fromBiprod_naturality`, read through
the comparison, natural by `h'OpensIsoH_naturality`. This is part of the functoriality clause of
[Stacks, Tag 0A50], which is stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem absoluteFromBiprod_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f) ≫
        absoluteFromBiprod G U V n =
      absoluteFromBiprod F U V n ≫ opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n f := by
  have key := comp_inv_eq_inv_comp_of_comp_hom_eq _ _ _ _
    (biprodIsoH_naturality F G U V f n)
  dsimp only [absoluteFromBiprod]
  simp only [Category.assoc]
  rw [reassoc_of% key, fromBiprod_naturality_assoc, cohomologyX₁IsoH_naturality]

/-- **The connecting morphisms of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence of étale cohomology groups are
natural in the abelian sheaf.** This is part of the functoriality clause of
[Stacks, Tag 0A50], which is stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`; the
other part is naturality of the restriction maps, `absoluteToBiprod_naturality` and
`absoluteFromBiprod_naturality`. -/
theorem absoluteDelta_naturality (f : F ⟶ G) (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n₀ f ≫ absoluteDelta G U V n₀ n₁ h =
      absoluteDelta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫ opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊔ V) n₁ f := by
  have hδ := delta_naturality F G U V f n₀ n₁ h
  have key := comp_inv_eq_inv_comp_of_comp_hom_eq _ _ _ _
    (cohomologyX₁IsoH_naturality F G U V f n₀)
  dsimp only [absoluteDelta]
  simp only [Category.assoc]
  rw [reassoc_of% key, ← cohomologyX₄IsoH_naturality F G U V f n₁, reassoc_of% hδ]

/-- **Stacks 0A50: Mayer--Vietoris for étale cohomology.** For open subschemes `U`, `V` of a
scheme `X` and an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X`, the sequence

`0 ⟶ H⁰(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U ∩ V, F)
  ⟶ H¹(U ∪ V, F) ⟶ H¹(U, F) ⊞ H¹(V, F) ⟶ H¹(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ ⋯`

of absolute étale cohomology groups of the open subschemes `U ∪ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ∩ V` is a long
exact sequence, functorial in `F`. That is the conjunction of the clauses stated here:

* its initial segment is exact, leading zero included, which is exactness at `H⁰(U ∪ V, F)`, the
  one place no six-term window reaches;
* every six-term window of it is exact, which is exactness at `Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)` and at
  `Hⁿ(U ∩ V, F)` for every `n`, and at `Hⁿ(U ∪ V, F)` for every `n ≥ 1`;
* each of the three families of maps of the sequence — the connecting morphisms, the sum of the
  two restriction maps and their difference — is natural in `F`, so that a morphism of abelian
  sheaves induces a morphism of long exact sequences: this is the functoriality the source
  asserts.

The first two clauses together are exactness at every term of the sequence.

The terms are the étale cohomology of the *schemes* `U ∪ V`, `U`, `V`, `U ∩ V` with coefficients
in the restriction of `F` to their small étale sites. The comparison with the cohomology over
those opens read as objects of the small étale site of `X`, which is where the exactness is
proved, is `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.h'OpensIsoH`, and the arrows of the sequence are
the arrows of `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence` read through
it.

The statement is made for arbitrary open subschemes `U`, `V`, so it is more general than the
source, whose hypothesis is `X = U ∪ V`: in that case the open subscheme `U ⊔ V` is `X` itself,
and the same ambient terms are identified with the global étale cohomology groups `Hⁿ(F)` on the
small étale site of `X` directly by
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySupIsoH`. -/
@[stacks 0A50]
theorem mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology :
    (absoluteInitialSequence F U V).Exact ∧
      (∀ (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁), (absoluteSequence F U V n₀ n₁ h).Exact) ∧
      (∀ (G' : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (f : F ⟶ G') (n₀ n₁ : ℕ)
          (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁),
        opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n₀ f ≫ absoluteDelta G' U V n₀ n₁ h =
          absoluteDelta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫ opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊔ V) n₁ f) ∧
      (∀ (G' : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (f : F ⟶ G') (n : ℕ),
        opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊔ V) n f ≫ absoluteToBiprod G' U V n =
          absoluteToBiprod F U V n ≫
            biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f)) ∧
      ∀ (G' : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (f : F ⟶ G') (n : ℕ),
        biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f) ≫
            absoluteFromBiprod G' U V n =
          absoluteFromBiprod F U V n ≫ opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n f :=
  ⟨absoluteInitialSequence_exact F U V,
    fun n₀ n₁ h => absoluteSequence_exact F U V n₀ n₁ h,
    fun G' f n₀ n₁ h => absoluteDelta_naturality F G' U V f n₀ n₁ h,
    fun G' f n => absoluteToBiprod_naturality F G' U V f n,
    fun G' f n => absoluteFromBiprod_naturality F G' U V f n⟩

end Sequence

/-! ### The sequence under the source's hypothesis that the two opens cover the scheme -/

section Cover

variable (X : Scheme.{u})

/-- **The map `Hⁿ(X, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(X, G)` on the global étale cohomology of `X`** induced by a
morphism `F ⟶ G` of abelian sheaves on the small étale site of `X`.

This is `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.functorH` acting on a morphism; as with
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.opensCohomologyMap` it is given a name so that its source
and target are spelled as the groups `Hⁿ` themselves, which is what lets the composites below be
recognised by `simp`. -/
def globalCohomologyMap (n : ℕ) {F G : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}} (f : F ⟶ G) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} F n) ⟶ AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} G n) :=
  (Sheaf.functorH.{u + 1, u + 1, u + 1, u + 1} (topology X) n).map f

end Cover

section CoverSequence

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} (F G : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (U V : X.Opens)

/-- **The ambient term of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is the global étale cohomology of `X`,
when the two opens cover `X`.** For `U ⊔ V = ⊤` the étale cohomology of the open subscheme
`U ∪ V` with coefficients in the restriction of `F` is the global étale cohomology `Hⁿ(X, F)`.

This is the composite of the two identifications the library already has:
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologyX₄IsoH`, which holds for arbitrary opens and
goes through the small étale site of the scheme `U ∪ V`, and
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.cohomologySupIsoH`, which uses `U ⊔ V = ⊤` to make the
right-hand object of the Mayer--Vietoris square terminal. -/
def supIsoGlobalH (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (n : ℕ) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊔ V)).obj F) n) ≅
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} F n) :=
  (cohomologyX₄IsoH F U V n).symm ≪≫ cohomologySupIsoH X U V F hUV n

/-- **The identification of the ambient term with global cohomology is natural in the abelian
sheaf.** Cohomology over a terminal object is global cohomology already as functors of the
sheaf, `Stacks.Cohomology.TerminalCohomology.functorHIso`, so this is
`CategoryTheory.NatTrans.naturality`. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem cohomologySupIsoH_naturality (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    ((Sheaf.cohomologyPresheafFunctor (topology X) n).map f).app (op (square X U V).X₄) ≫
        (cohomologySupIsoH X U V G hUV n).hom =
      (cohomologySupIsoH X U V F hUV n).hom ≫ globalCohomologyMap X n f :=
  (Stacks.Cohomology.TerminalCohomology.functorHIso (topology X) (square X U V).X₄
    (squareX₄IsTerminal X U V hUV) n).hom.naturality f

/-- **The comparison `Hⁿ(U ∪ V, F) ≅ Hⁿ(X, F)` is natural in the abelian sheaf**: it is the
composite of two comparisons each natural in the sheaf, `cohomologyX₄IsoH_naturality` and
`cohomologySupIsoH_naturality`. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem supIsoGlobalH_naturality (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊔ V) n f ≫ (supIsoGlobalH G U V hUV n).hom =
      (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n).hom ≫ globalCohomologyMap X n f := by
  have key := comp_inv_eq_inv_comp_of_comp_hom_eq _ _ _ _
    (cohomologyX₄IsoH_naturality F G U V f n)
  dsimp only [supIsoGlobalH]
  simp only [Iso.trans_hom, Iso.symm_hom, Category.assoc]
  rw [reassoc_of% key, cohomologySupIsoH_naturality F G U V hUV f n]

/-- The sum of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(X, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)`, for `X = U ∪ V`: the
corresponding map `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.absoluteToBiprod` of the sequence of the
open subschemes, with its source read as the global étale cohomology of `X` through
`supIsoGlobalH`. -/
def coverToBiprod (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (n : ℕ) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} F n) ⟶
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X U).obj F) n) ⊞
        AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X V).obj F) n) :=
  (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n).inv ≫ absoluteToBiprod F U V n

/-- **The connecting morphism `δ : Hⁿ⁰(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(X, F)`**, for `X = U ∪ V`: the connecting
morphism `Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.absoluteDelta` with its target read as the global
étale cohomology of `X` through `supIsoGlobalH`. -/
def coverDelta (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} ((opensRestriction X (U ⊓ V)).obj F) n₀) ⟶
      AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} F n₁) :=
  absoluteDelta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫ (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n₁).hom

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris sequence of a scheme covered by two opens.** For `X = U ∪ V` a union of
two open subschemes, an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X` and `n₀ + 1 = n₁`, this
is the six-term window

`Hⁿ⁰(X, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁰(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ⁰(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁰(U ∩ V, F)
  ⟶ Hⁿ¹(X, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ¹(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(U ∩ V, F)`

of the long exact sequence, with the ambient terms the global étale cohomology groups of `X`
itself — the terms [Stacks, Tag 0A50] names. -/
abbrev coverSequence (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    ComposableArrows AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} 5 :=
  ComposableArrows.mk₅ (coverToBiprod F U V hUV n₀) (absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₀)
    (coverDelta F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h) (coverToBiprod F U V hUV n₁) (absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₁)

/-- The comparison of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence of the open subschemes with the sequence whose
ambient terms are the global étale cohomology groups of `X`: the ambient terms are compared by
`supIsoGlobalH`, the other terms are the same, and the arrows of the second sequence are by
definition those of the first read through that comparison. -/
def coverSequenceIso (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    absoluteSequence F U V n₀ n₁ h ≅ coverSequence F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h :=
  ComposableArrows.isoMk₅ (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n₀) (Iso.refl _) (Iso.refl _)
    (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n₁) (Iso.refl _) (Iso.refl _)
    (by
      change absoluteToBiprod F U V n₀ ≫ (Iso.refl _).hom =
        (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n₀).hom ≫ coverToBiprod F U V hUV n₀
      simp [coverToBiprod])
    (by
      change absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₀ ≫ (Iso.refl _).hom =
        (Iso.refl _).hom ≫ absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₀
      simp)
    (by
      change absoluteDelta F U V n₀ n₁ h ≫ (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n₁).hom =
        (Iso.refl _).hom ≫ coverDelta F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h
      simp [coverDelta])
    (by
      change absoluteToBiprod F U V n₁ ≫ (Iso.refl _).hom =
        (supIsoGlobalH F U V hUV n₁).hom ≫ coverToBiprod F U V hUV n₁
      simp [coverToBiprod])
    (by
      change absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₁ ≫ (Iso.refl _).hom =
        (Iso.refl _).hom ≫ absoluteFromBiprod F U V n₁
      simp)

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris sequence of a scheme covered by two opens is exact**: for `X = U ∪ V`
and an abelian sheaf `F` on the small étale site of `X`, every six-term window of

`⋯ ⟶ Hⁿ(X, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ⁺¹(X, F) ⟶ ⋯`

is exact. This is one of the three clauses of [Stacks, Tag 0A50] as the source states it, which
is stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_cover`. -/
theorem coverSequence_exact (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    (coverSequence F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h).Exact :=
  ComposableArrows.exact_of_iso (coverSequenceIso F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h)
    (absoluteSequence_exact F U V n₀ n₁ h)

/-- The initial segment

`0 ⟶ H⁰(X, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U ∩ V, F)
  ⟶ H¹(X, F) ⟶ H¹(U, F) ⊞ H¹(V, F) ⟶ H¹(U ∩ V, F)`

of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence of a scheme covered by two opens, with its leading zero. -/
abbrev coverInitialSequence (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) : ComposableArrows AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1} 6 :=
  (coverSequence F U V hUV 0 1 rfl).precomp
    (0 : AddCommGrpCat.of PUnit.{u + 2} ⟶ AddCommGrpCat.of (Sheaf.H.{u + 1} F 0))

/-- The comparison of the two initial segments, extending `coverSequenceIso` by the identity of
the leading zero. -/
def coverInitialSequenceIso (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) :
    absoluteInitialSequence F U V ≅ coverInitialSequence F U V hUV :=
  ComposableArrows.isoMkSucc (Iso.refl _) (coverSequenceIso F U V hUV 0 1 rfl) (by simp)

/-- **The Mayer--Vietoris sequence of a scheme covered by two opens is exact at its initial
segment, leading zero included**: the sum of the two restriction maps
`H⁰(X, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F)` is injective. This is one of the three clauses of
[Stacks, Tag 0A50] as the source states it, which is stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_cover`. -/
theorem coverInitialSequence_exact (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) :
    (coverInitialSequence F U V hUV).Exact :=
  ComposableArrows.exact_of_iso (coverInitialSequenceIso F U V hUV)
    (absoluteInitialSequence_exact F U V)

/-- **The sum of the two restriction maps `Hⁿ(X, F) ⟶ Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)` is natural in the
abelian sheaf.** It is the corresponding map of the sequence of the open subschemes, natural by
`absoluteToBiprod_naturality`, read through the comparison of the ambient terms, natural by
`supIsoGlobalH_naturality`. This is part of the functoriality clause of [Stacks, Tag 0A50],
stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_cover`. -/
@[reassoc]
theorem coverToBiprod_naturality (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (f : F ⟶ G) (n : ℕ) :
    globalCohomologyMap X n f ≫ coverToBiprod G U V hUV n =
      coverToBiprod F U V hUV n ≫
        biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f) := by
  have key := comp_inv_eq_inv_comp_of_comp_hom_eq _ _ _ _
    (supIsoGlobalH_naturality F G U V hUV f n)
  dsimp only [coverToBiprod]
  simp only [Category.assoc]
  rw [reassoc_of% key, absoluteToBiprod_naturality]

/-- **The connecting morphisms `Hⁿ⁰(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ Hⁿ¹(X, F)` are natural in the abelian sheaf.**
They are the connecting morphisms of the sequence of the open subschemes, natural by
`absoluteDelta_naturality`, read through the comparison of the ambient terms, natural by
`supIsoGlobalH_naturality`. This is part of the functoriality clause of [Stacks, Tag 0A50],
stated whole in
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_cover`. -/
theorem coverDelta_naturality (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) (f : F ⟶ G) (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁) :
    opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n₀ f ≫ coverDelta G U V hUV n₀ n₁ h =
      coverDelta F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h ≫ globalCohomologyMap X n₁ f := by
  dsimp only [coverDelta]
  simp only [Category.assoc]
  rw [reassoc_of% absoluteDelta_naturality F G U V f n₀ n₁ h,
    supIsoGlobalH_naturality F G U V hUV f n₁]

/-- **Stacks 0A50: Mayer--Vietoris for étale cohomology, as the source states it.** Let `X` be a
scheme which is the union `X = U ∪ V` of two open subschemes and let `F` be an abelian sheaf on
the small étale site of `X`. Then

`0 ⟶ H⁰(X, F) ⟶ H⁰(U, F) ⊞ H⁰(V, F) ⟶ H⁰(U ∩ V, F)
  ⟶ H¹(X, F) ⟶ H¹(U, F) ⊞ H¹(V, F) ⟶ H¹(U ∩ V, F) ⟶ ⋯`

is a long exact sequence, functorial in `F`. That is the conjunction of the clauses stated here:

* its initial segment is exact, leading zero included, which is exactness at `H⁰(X, F)`, the one
  place no six-term window reaches;
* every six-term window of it is exact, which is exactness at `Hⁿ(U, F) ⊞ Hⁿ(V, F)` and at
  `Hⁿ(U ∩ V, F)` for every `n`, and at `Hⁿ(X, F)` for every `n ≥ 1`;
* each of the three families of maps of the sequence — the connecting morphisms, the sum of the
  two restriction maps and their difference — is natural in `F`, so that a morphism of abelian
  sheaves induces a morphism of long exact sequences: this is the functoriality the source
  asserts.

The first two clauses together are exactness at every term of the sequence.

The ambient terms are the global étale cohomology groups of `X` itself, which is the hypothesis
`U ⊔ V = ⊤` being used: the other terms are the étale cohomology of the schemes `U`, `V`,
`U ∩ V` with coefficients in the restriction of `F` to their small étale sites.

This is the statement of
`Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris.mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_etaleCohomology`, which
makes no hypothesis on `U` and `V` and whose ambient terms are therefore the étale cohomology of
the open subscheme `U ∪ V`, transported along the comparison `supIsoGlobalH` of those terms with
the global étale cohomology of `X`. Nothing of the Mayer--Vietoris sequence is reproved. -/
@[stacks 0A50 "the source's own statement: the hypothesis `X = U ∪ V`, with the ambient terms \
of the sequence the global étale cohomology groups of `X` itself"]
theorem mayerVietoris_longExactSequence_cover (hUV : U ⊔ V = ⊤) :
    (coverInitialSequence F U V hUV).Exact ∧
      (∀ (n₀ n₁ : ℕ) (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁), (coverSequence F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h).Exact) ∧
      (∀ (G' : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (f : F ⟶ G') (n₀ n₁ : ℕ)
          (h : n₀ + 1 = n₁),
        opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n₀ f ≫ coverDelta G' U V hUV n₀ n₁ h =
          coverDelta F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h ≫ globalCohomologyMap X n₁ f) ∧
      (∀ (G' : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (f : F ⟶ G') (n : ℕ),
        globalCohomologyMap X n f ≫ coverToBiprod G' U V hUV n =
          coverToBiprod F U V hUV n ≫
            biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f)) ∧
      ∀ (G' : Sheaf (topology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u + 1}) (f : F ⟶ G') (n : ℕ),
        biprod.map (opensCohomologyMap X U n f) (opensCohomologyMap X V n f) ≫
            absoluteFromBiprod G' U V n =
          absoluteFromBiprod F U V n ≫ opensCohomologyMap X (U ⊓ V) n f :=
  ⟨coverInitialSequence_exact F U V hUV,
    fun n₀ n₁ h => coverSequence_exact F U V hUV n₀ n₁ h,
    fun G' f n₀ n₁ h => coverDelta_naturality F G' U V hUV f n₀ n₁ h,
    fun G' f n => coverToBiprod_naturality F G' U V hUV f n,
    fun G' f n => absoluteFromBiprod_naturality F G' U V f n⟩

end CoverSequence

end Stacks.EtaleCohomology.MayerVietoris

